  ],
  "batch_notes": "Three recurring shapes the rubric handles awkwardly. (1) 'Generated model' tests (dep3 headers, dyn-fmt pieces, dynfmt curly, distro-info CSV, edn-rs radix): the generator builds the input and the expected answer together; I used model-oracle when a model object/function computes expectations from structure (dep3 `h.ordered_fields()`, dyn-fmt `expected(pieces,args)`, dynfmt curly model) and spec-postcondition when the expected value is simply the generated value (CSV fields, integers) — the boundary is fuzzy and other classifiers may split it differently. (2) Wrapper-vs-primitive tests (decorum vs f64, dyon string concat vs Rust): the rubric says stdlib = external-oracle, which I followed, but these are weaker than a true independent implementation. (3) KNOWN FAILURE markers: euclid's hegel_positive_is_in_zero_two_pi and hegel_intersects_iff_intersection_is_some are general, un-narrowed properties that happen to fail; I tagged them pin per the CLASSIFY rule (doc says KNOWN FAILURE) but flagged in notes — excluding them from bug rates would undercount genuine finds. Similarly debversion's bug-comment properties (e.g. bin_nmu_count_reads_the_last_suffix, nmu_count_survives_a_bin_nmu) are moderately narrowed generative pins. Two `doc_examples` tests (dyn-fmt, dynfmt) draw nothing and are example tests wrapped in #[hegel::test]; classified `other`. edn-rs has two known_bug_* pins (whole_double, control_char) with no direct bug link because edn-rs/4 bundles three manifestations under one record naming only the unicode-whitespace test. Bug records with empty `test` (dyon/2, edn-rs/3) were attributed via the property whose comment gates around the hang/overflow. distro-info's whole suite is C-tool differential; debversion mixes dpkg/apt/python-debian/git as oracles (git check-ref-format is the notable one). dep3's 'inherited' deb822-lossless/9 gate in paragraph_edits_are_seen_by_the_getters is not a bug in this target's list."
}
